WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.580
If you remove most of a barley leaf's pores, does the plant go hungry?

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.080
Or can it save water and still fill its grain?

3
00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:16.563
On paper, a leaf with fewer pores can pay less water for its carbon.

4
00:00:17.863 --> 00:00:21.263
A real experiment tested what fewer pores do to a crop.

5
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:26.133
Julie Gray's team, with Hughes as first author, worked in barley.

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:31.693
They made plants overexpress a barley gene, HvEPF1.

7
00:00:32.973 --> 00:00:35.213
That means making extra of the gene's product.

8
00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:41.217
This gene limits leaf cells from entering stomatal development and moving through

9
00:00:41.217 --> 00:00:44.937
it. So extra product should mean fewer stomata.

10
00:00:46.217 --> 00:00:48.877
They compared these plants with empty-vector controls.

11
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.490
Leaf surface impressions told the story.

12
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:58.430
Two engineered lines kept only about a quarter and an eighth of the controls'

13
00:00:58.430 --> 00:00:59.430
stomata.

14
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:02.133
Why the controls, and why two lines?

15
00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.393
The procedure itself might change a leaf.

16
00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:09.493
And the inserted gene lands somewhere in the genome.

17
00:01:10.753 --> 00:01:14.633
Two independent lines make a landing-spot effect less likely.

18
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:20.273
Next, a leaf was clamped in a small chamber that measures gas exchange.

19
00:01:21.653 --> 00:01:26.773
In well-watered plants, leaves with fewer stomata exchanged much less gas with the

20
00:01:26.773 --> 00:01:27.773
air.

21
00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:29.993
Gas exchange fell steeply.

22
00:01:31.113 --> 00:01:34.053
So what happened to the carbon taken in, and to the grain?

23
00:01:35.333 --> 00:01:36.833
Did they fall just as steeply?

24
00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:49.030
In general, if fewer pores slow gas flow while photosynthesis continues, carbon

25
00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:50.910
dioxide inside the leaf falls.

26
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.930
Its gap widens, and that props up carbon gain.

27
00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.050
So a leaf can save more water than it loses carbon.

28
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:05.590
In this study, one efficiency measure rose in both lines under water restriction.

29
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.470
The measures did not all agree.

30
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:15.833
the grain? In a greenhouse trial, neither line showed a statistically significant

31
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:19.153
drop in yield, well watered or water restricted.

32
00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:21.853
No drop was detected.

33
00:02:22.917 --> 00:02:25.117
But each group held only five plants.

34
00:02:26.297 --> 00:02:29.557
A small loss could still hide below what the trial could see.

35
00:02:30.797 --> 00:02:33.657
No drop detected is not proof of equal yield.

36
00:02:34.717 --> 00:02:36.557
So what kind of answer is this?

37
00:02:37.637 --> 00:02:43.117
It shows how one gene limits stomata, and what fewer stomata do to gas, water and

38
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:48.817
grain. It says nothing about why wild barley evolved its stomatal density.

39
00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:54.437
That second question is about lineages and their history, not one plant.

40
00:02:55.837 --> 00:02:59.737
Answering it needs different evidence, and it is where the lesson goes next.
